168	2	5e3c6e4cce15	/projects/talavo	820	Add favicon caching for custom shortcuts	Add favicon caching for custom shortcuts	failed	\N	doohub/issue-820-add-favicon-caching-for-custom-shortcuts	832	https://github.com/bakicelebi/talavo/pull/832	Pipeline failed after max review rounds for issue #820	0	claude-opus-4-6	0	\N	2026-03-15 10:29:13.299959+00	2026-03-15 10:35:25.610619+00	24	[{"error": null, "output": null, "status": "completed", "node_id": "start", "node_name": "Start", "node_type": "start", "duration_s": 0.1, "started_at": "2026-03-15T10:29:14.673256+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-15T10:29:14.726221+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "blocker_status=READY, blocker_reason=Issue #820 has no blocking language in its description, and no other open issues reference it as a dependency. It's a self-contained enhancement to add favicon caching for custom shortcuts.\\n\\n**READY**", "status": "completed", "node_id": "check_blockers", "node_name": "Check Blockers", "node_type": "claude_agent", "duration_s": 11.3, "started_at": "2026-03-15T10:29:14.781006+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-15T10:29:26.109848+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "blocker_status=READY → check_clarity", "status": "completed", "node_id": "blocker_gate", "node_name": "Blocker Gate", "node_type": "condition", "duration_s": 0.1, "started_at": "2026-03-15T10:29:26.159280+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-15T10:29:26.209954+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "clarity_status=CLEAR, clarity_reason=**CLEAR**\\n\\nThe issue is understandable to a competent iOS developer:\\n\\n- **What:** Custom shortcuts currently don't cache favicons for user-added URLs\\n- **Why:** To improve the visual experience (show icons alongside shortcuts)\\n- **Scope:** Small, focused task — fetch and cache favicons for custom shortcut URLs\\n\\nA developer can reasonably infer the implementation: fetch the favicon (e.g., `https://domain.com/favicon.ico` or via Google's favicon service), store it locally (UserDefaults, file cache, or similar), and display it in the shortcuts UI. No critical ambiguity blocks implementation.", "status": "completed", "node_id": "check_clarity", "node_name": "Check Clarity", "node_type": "claude_agent", "duration_s": 13.5, "started_at": "2026-03-15T10:29:26.256466+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-15T10:29:39.721445+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "clarity_status=CLEAR → analyze", "status": "completed", "node_id": "clarity_gate", "node_name": "Clarity Gate", "node_type": "condition", "duration_s": 0.1, "started_at": "2026-03-15T10:29:39.792294+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-15T10:29:39.842493+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "project_context=---\\n\\n## Project Context: Issue #820 — Favicon Caching for Custom Shortcuts\\n\\n### The Problem\\n\\n`fetchFavicon(for:)` in `ShortcutsStore.swift` **never actually fetches anything** — it just constructs the Google favicon URL string and stores it. `CustomShortcutCard` then uses `AsyncImage` which re-fetches from the network on every render. There's no persistent binary caching.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Relevant Files\\n\\n| File | Purpose |\\n|------|---------|\\n| `talavo/Features/Shortcuts/Store/ShortcutsStore.swift` | `ShortcutItem` model + store — **primary file to change** |\\n| `talavo/Features/Home/Widgets/CustomShortcutCard.swift` | Renders the shortcut card with `AsyncImage` — may need update |\\n| `talavo/Features/History/Store/HistoryStore.swift` | Reference: same pattern (URL string favicon), but simpler (no caching needed there since history is ephemeral) |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Current Code Behavior\\n\\n**`ShortcutsStore.fetchFavicon()`** (line 46–52):\\n- Constructs `https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain={host}&sz=64`\\n- Immediately saves that URL string into `shortcut.favicon`\\n- **Does not make any HTTP request**\\n\\n**`CustomShortcutCard`** (line 16–24):\\n- Reads `shortcut.favicon` URL string → passes to `AsyncImage(url:)`\\n- SwiftUI's `AsyncImage` uses `URLCache` internally, but this cache is not persistent across cold launches and is controlled by HTTP headers from Google's service\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Coding Patterns to Follow\\n\\n1. **UserDefaults persistence** — all stores use `JSONEncoder`/`JSONDecoder` into `UserDefaults`. Binary data (`Data`) is `Codable`, so `favicon` can be changed from `String?` to `Data?` without changing the persistence mechanism.\\n\\n2. **ObservableObject + @Published** — `ShortcutsStore` is an `ObservableObject`. Mutations happen on main thread via `@Published var shortcuts`. Any async URLSession callback must dispatch to `DispatchQueue.main`.\\n\\n3. **Silent failure** — errors don't surface to the user; fall back to the globe icon.\\n\\n4. **`TapScaleButtonStyle`** and **`GlassCard`** are the UI primitives used in shortcut cards — don't change those.\\n\\n5. **URLSession direct** — no Alamofire. Use `URLSession.shared.dataTask(with:)`.\\n\\n6. **Google favicon service** — already established: `https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain={host}&sz=64`\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Implementation Path\\n\\n- Change `ShortcutItem.favicon` from `String?` to `Data?` — stores the actual image bytes\\n- In `fetchFavicon(for:)`, perform a real `URLSession` data task, then on success store the response `Data` into the shortcut and call `saveShortcuts()`\\n- In `CustomShortcutCard`, replace `AsyncImage(url:)` with `Image(uiImage: UIImage(data: favicon))` when `favicon: Data?` is non-nil\\n- Skip re-fetching if `shortcut.favicon != nil` already (idempotent on re-add)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Key Technical Considerations\\n\\n- **`Data` is `Codable`** — UserDefaults stores it as base64 in the JSON blob. Works fine for small favicon PNGs (~1–4 KB each).\\n- **Migration** — existing users have `favicon: String?` saved in UserDefaults. Changing the type to `Data?` will silently fail to decode (nil), triggering a re-fetch on next launch. This is fine — the app already handles `nil` favicon gracefully.\\n- **`updateShortcut`** currently doesn't re-fetch the favicon when the URL changes (line 34–39). That should also call `fetchFavicon` if the URL host changes.\\n- **Thread safety** — `URLSession` callbacks are on a background thread; must dispatch updates to `DispatchQueue.main` before mutating `@Published` array.", "status": "completed", "node_id": "analyze", "node_name": "Analyze Project", "node_type": "claude_agent", "duration_s": 92.3, "started_at": "2026-03-15T10:29:39.899044+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-15T10:31:12.224956+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "plan=I now have a complete picture. Here's the implementation plan:\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Implementation Plan: #820 — Favicon Caching for Custom Shortcuts\\n\\n### Overview\\n\\nChange `ShortcutItem.favicon` from `String?` (URL) to `Data?` (cached image bytes). Make `fetchFavicon` perform an actual HTTP request and store the response data. Update UI to render from `Data` instead of `AsyncImage`. Mirrors the Flutter implementation in `shortcuts_bloc.dart`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Files to Modify\\n\\n#### 1. `talavo-ios/talavo/Features/Shortcuts/Store/ShortcutsStore.swift`\\n\\n**Model change:**\\n- Change `ShortcutItem.favicon` from `String?` to `Data?`\\n- Update the `init` parameter type accordingly\\n\\n**`fetchFavicon(for:)` — rewrite:**\\n- Construct the Google favicon URL as before\\n- Perform `URLSession.shared.dataTask(with:)` to actually fetch the image bytes\\n- On success (HTTP 200, non-empty data): find the shortcut by ID, set `.favicon = responseData`, call `saveShortcuts()`\\n- Dispatch UI update to `DispatchQueue.main`\\n- On failure: do nothing (silent failure — globe icon fallback remains)\\n\\n**`addShortcut` — no change needed** (already calls `fetchFavicon`)\\n\\n**`updateShortcut` — add favicon re-fetch:**\\n- Compare old URL host vs new URL host\\n- If host changed: clear `shortcuts[index].favicon = nil`, call `fetchFavicon(for: shortcuts[index])`\\n- If host unchanged: keep existing cached favicon\\n\\n**`loadShortcuts` — add startup re-fetch:**\\n- After loading, iterate shortcuts where `favicon == nil` and call `fetchFavicon` for each\\n- Handles migration: old `String?` favicons will fail to decode as `Data?`, resulting in `nil`, triggering a fresh fetch\\n\\n#### 2. `talavo-ios/talavo/Features/Home/Widgets/CustomShortcutCard.swift`\\n\\n- Replace the `AsyncImage` block (lines 16–24) with:\\n  - If `shortcut.favicon` is non-nil, create `UIImage(data:)` and display via `Image(uiImage:).resizable()`\\n  - If `UIImage(data:)` returns nil (corrupt data), fall back to globe icon\\n  - If `shortcut.favicon` is nil, show globe icon (existing else branch)\\n- Remove the `placeholder:` closure (no longer needed — image is either cached or not)\\n\\n#### 3. `talavo-ios/talavo/Features/Home/Widgets/ReorderableShortcutGrid.swift`\\n\\n- Update the `.shortcut` case in `cardContent(for:)` (lines 193–207):\\n  - Same pattern as CustomShortcutCard: replace `AsyncImage(url:)` with `Image(uiImage: UIImage(data:))`\\n  - Fall back to globe icon when favicon is nil or data is invalid\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Edge Cases & Risks\\n\\n- **Migration**: Existing users have `favicon: String?` in UserDefaults JSON. Changing to `Data?` means `JSONDecoder` will produce `nil` for the old string values. The startup re-fetch loop handles this automatically.\\n- **UserDefaults size**: Favicons at 64×64 are ~1–4 KB each. Even 50 shortcuts = ~200 KB — well within UserDefaults limits.\\n- **Concurrent fetches**: Multiple `dataTask` callbacks could race. Mitigation: each callback finds the shortcut by ID and dispatches to main thread — only the array mutation needs to be on main, which serializes updates naturally.\\n- **Network unavailable**: Silent failure. Shortcuts render with globe icon. Next app launch retries via the `loadShortcuts` re-fetch loop.\\n- **Invalid image data**: `UIImage(data:)` returns `nil` for non-image responses (e.g., HTML error pages). Treat as failure — show globe icon, don't cache. Add a guard: only store data if `UIImage(data: responseData) != nil`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Implementation Order\\n\\n1. Update `ShortcutItem` model (`favicon: String?` → `Data?`)\\n2. Rewrite `fetchFavicon(for:)` with real `URLSession` fetch\\n3. Add host-change detection to `updateShortcut`\\n4. Add nil-favicon re-fetch loop to `loadShortcuts`\\n5. Update `CustomShortcutCard` UI\\n6. Update `ReorderableShortcutGrid` UI\\n7. Run `dart format .` (if any Dart touched) / build & test on simulator", "status": "completed", "node_id": "plan", "node_name": "Plan", "node_type": "claude_agent", "duration_s": 87.1, "started_at": "2026-03-15T10:31:12.281012+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-15T10:32:39.345910+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "dev_result=\\n\\nAll changes are already implemented and the PR is open. The implementation is complete:\\n\\n1. **ShortcutsStore.swift** — `favicon: Data?`, real `URLSession` fetch with `[weak self]`, host-change detection in `updateShortcut`, nil-favicon re-fetch on `loadShortcuts`\\n2. **CustomShortcutCard.swift** — `Image(uiImage:)` from cached `Data` instead of `AsyncImage`\\n3. **ReorderableShortcutGrid.swift** — Same cached `Data` rendering pattern\\n\\nhttps://github.com/bakicelebi/talavo/pull/832", "status": "completed", "node_id": "develop", "node_name": "Develop", "node_type": "claude_agent", "duration_s": 10.3, "started_at": "2026-03-15T10:34:38.976850+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-15T10:34:49.295467+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "review_result=**CHANGES_REQUESTED**\\n\\nThe implementation is clean overall — good use of `[weak self]`, proper host-change detection on update, and the view code is simple and correct. One issue to fix:\\n\\n### 1. Force unwrap on favicon URL (line 61, `ShortcutsStore.swift`)\\n\\n```swift\\nlet faviconUrl = URL(\\n    string: \\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=\\\\(host)&sz=64\\")!\\n```\\n\\nThis violates the \\"no force unwraps\\" rule. While the URL pattern is predictable, `host` is interpolated and could theoretically contain characters that break URL parsing. Use a `guard let` instead:\\n\\n```swift\\nguard let faviconUrl = URL(\\n    string: \\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=\\\\(host)&sz=64\\") else { return }\\n```\\n\\nEverything else looks good:\\n- `[weak self]` in the URLSession closure with `shortcutId` captured by value — no retain cycle\\n- Favicon cleared and re-fetched when the host changes on edit — correct behavior\\n- Missing favicons fetched on load — good resilience\\n- Views use `GlassCard`, `AppTheme` colors, and stay well under 200 lines\\n- `try?` on encode/decode follows existing UserDefaults patterns, review_verdict=CHANGES_REQUESTED", "status": "completed", "node_id": "review", "node_name": "Review", "node_type": "claude_agent", "duration_s": 36.0, "started_at": "2026-03-15T10:34:49.380713+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-15T10:35:25.411392+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "review_verdict=CHANGES_REQUESTED → failed", "status": "completed", "node_id": "review_gate", "node_name": "Review Gate", "node_type": "condition", "duration_s": 0.1, "started_at": "2026-03-15T10:35:25.465818+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-15T10:35:25.519092+00:00"}, {"error": "Pipeline failed after max review rounds for issue #820", "output": null, "status": "failed", "node_id": "failed", "node_name": "Failed", "node_type": "failed", "duration_s": 371.1, "started_at": "2026-03-15T10:35:25.572097+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-15T10:35:25.572105+00:00"}]	\N
191	1	f015857ce02e	/projects/talavo	878	Speed pill should live-update video playback and persist across videos	Speed pill should live-update video playback and persist across videos	done	\N	doohub/issue-878-speed-pill-should-live-update-video-play	891	https://github.com/bakicelebi/talavo/pull/891	\N	0	claude-opus-4-6	0	\N	2026-03-16 22:01:18.333725+00	2026-03-16 22:06:51.129431+00	24	[{"error": null, "output": null, "status": "completed", "node_id": "start", "node_name": "Start", "node_type": "start", "duration_s": 0.1, "started_at": "2026-03-16T22:01:19.744639+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-16T22:01:19.799852+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "blocker_status=READY, blocker_reason=Issue #878 contains no \\"blocked by\\", \\"depends on\\", or dependency language. It's a self-contained feature (inject `playbackRate` JS into WebViews) with no references to other issues.\\n\\n**READY**\\n\\nIssue #878 has no dependencies on other open issues and can be worked on immediately.", "status": "completed", "node_id": "check_blockers", "node_name": "Check Blockers", "node_type": "claude_agent", "duration_s": 20.1, "started_at": "2026-03-16T22:01:19.871969+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-16T22:01:39.946734+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "blocker_status=READY → check_clarity", "status": "completed", "node_id": "blocker_gate", "node_name": "Blocker Gate", "node_type": "condition", "duration_s": 0.1, "started_at": "2026-03-16T22:01:40.043698+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-16T22:01:40.126684+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "clarity_status=CLEAR, clarity_reason=**CLEAR**\\n\\nThe issue has well-defined requirements, current vs. expected behavior, and even specifies the implementation approach (JavaScript injection into WebViews). A competent iOS developer knows exactly what to build.", "status": "completed", "node_id": "check_clarity", "node_name": "Check Clarity", "node_type": "claude_agent", "duration_s": 13.8, "started_at": "2026-03-16T22:01:40.238259+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-16T22:01:54.022747+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "clarity_status=CLEAR → analyze", "status": "completed", "node_id": "clarity_gate", "node_name": "Clarity Gate", "node_type": "condition", "duration_s": 0.1, "started_at": "2026-03-16T22:01:54.137724+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-16T22:01:54.236749+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "project_context=## Project Context for Issue #878\\n\\n### Key Files\\n\\n| File | Purpose |\\n|---|---|\\n| `Features/PlaybackSpeed/Views/SpeedPill.swift` | Draggable/tappable pill UI — binds directly to `$settings.playbackSpeed` |\\n| `Features/PlaybackSpeed/Views/SpeedSelectorSheet.swift` | Full speed picker sheet, also writes to `settings.playbackSpeed` |\\n| `Features/Settings/Store/SettingsStore.swift:72` | `@Published var playbackSpeed: Double` — persists to `UserDefaults(\\"playback_speed\\")` via `didSet` |\\n| `Features/Browser/Services/VideoWebViewHolder.swift` | Owns the persistent YouTube video `WKWebView`; has `evaluateJavaScript(_ js: String)` |\\n| `Features/Browser/Services/ShortsWebViewHolder.swift` | Same pattern for Shorts WebView |\\n| `Features/Browser/Services/ScriptInjector.swift:650` | Where speed is currently applied — **only on page finish load**, not live |\\n| `Features/Browser/Views/BrowserScreen.swift` | Has `@EnvironmentObject` access to both holders; where `onChange` observer needs to go |\\n| `Features/Browser/Views/BrowserActionDrawerView.swift:173` | Where `SpeedPill` is embedded |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Root Cause\\n\\n`ScriptInjector.injectOnPageFinish()` only injects speed once when a page loads:\\n```swift\\n// ScriptInjector.swift ~line 650\\nif settings.playbackSpeed != 1.0 {\\n    let speedJs = \\"(function(){var v=document.querySelector('video');if(v)v.playbackRate=\\\\(settings.playbackSpeed);})()\\"\\n    webView.evaluateJavaScript(speedJs)\\n}\\n```\\nThere is **no `onChange` observer** for `settings.playbackSpeed` in `BrowserScreen.swift` — speed changes from the pill are never pushed to the live WebView.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Patterns to Follow\\n\\n- **Reactive updates:** `BrowserScreen` already uses `.onChange(of: someValue)` to react to setting changes — add the same for `settings.playbackSpeed`\\n- **JS injection at runtime:** Both holders expose `evaluateJavaScript()` for live injection — use the same pattern as `pauseVideo()`\\n- **On-load injection:** The existing `injectOnPageFinish()` code already handles new video loads — it just needs to apply speed regardless of whether it equals 1.0 (remove the `!= 1.0` guard)\\n- **Accessing holders:** `BrowserScreen` has both as `@EnvironmentObject` — no new DI wiring needed\\n\\n---\\n\\n### What Needs to Change\\n\\n1. **`BrowserScreen.swift`** — Add `.onChange(of: settings.playbackSpeed)` to call JS injection on both `videoWebViewHolder` and `shortsWebViewHolder`\\n2. **`ScriptInjector.swift:650`** — Remove the `!= 1.0` guard so speed is always applied on page load (even 1x is explicit)\\n3. Both holders likely need a small public helper (e.g. `setPlaybackRate(_ rate: Double)`) to keep JS strings out of the view layer — following the same pattern as the existing `pauseVideo()` method\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Technical Considerations\\n\\n- Both WebViews are **persistent, prewarm instances** — the video element exists across navigations; `evaluateJavaScript` will reach it directly\\n- YouTube Shorts may load video elements asynchronously — the JS should be tolerant of `null` (`if(v)`)\\n- The `ScriptInjector` receives `settings` at injection time; the fix for new-video persistence is straightforward since it already reads `settings.playbackSpeed`\\n- No new persistence work needed — `SettingsStore` already persists to `UserDefaults` automatically", "status": "completed", "node_id": "analyze", "node_name": "Analyze Project", "node_type": "claude_agent", "duration_s": 86.9, "started_at": "2026-03-16T22:01:54.327648+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-16T22:03:21.247737+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "plan=\\n\\n# Implementation Plan: Issue #878 — Speed Pill Live-Update & Persistence\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nThree surgical changes: (1) add a `setPlaybackRate` helper to both WebView holders, (2) add `.onChange` observer in `BrowserScreen` to push speed changes live, (3) remove the `!= 1.0` guard in `ScriptInjector` so every new video gets the correct speed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Changes\\n\\n### 1. `VideoWebViewHolder.swift` — Add `setPlaybackRate` method\\n\\n- Add a public method `setPlaybackRate(_ rate: Double)` that calls `evaluateJavaScript` with the speed-setting JS\\n- Follow the exact pattern of the existing `pauseVideo()` method\\n- JS should be null-safe: `document.querySelector('video')?.playbackRate = rate`\\n\\n### 2. `ShortsWebViewHolder.swift` — Add `setPlaybackRate` method\\n\\n- Identical method as above — same JS, same pattern\\n- Shorts may load video elements async, so the `if(v)` null guard is important\\n\\n### 3. `BrowserScreen.swift` — Add `.onChange(of: settings.playbackSpeed)` observer\\n\\n- Add a single `.onChange(of: settings.playbackSpeed)` modifier to the view body\\n- In the closure, call `videoWebViewHolder.setPlaybackRate(newValue)` and `shortsWebViewHolder.setPlaybackRate(newValue)`\\n- Both holders are already available as `@EnvironmentObject` — no new wiring needed\\n- Place the modifier alongside the existing `.onChange` modifiers for consistency\\n\\n### 4. `ScriptInjector.swift` (~line 650) — Remove `!= 1.0` guard\\n\\n- Change `if settings.playbackSpeed != 1.0 {` to always execute the speed injection\\n- This ensures that when a user sets speed back to 1.0x, new videos respect that instead of potentially inheriting a non-1x rate from a previous injection\\n- The JS string itself stays the same\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Files Summary\\n\\n| File | Action | Change |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `Features/Browser/Services/VideoWebViewHolder.swift` | Modify | Add `setPlaybackRate(_:)` method |\\n| `Features/Browser/Services/ShortsWebViewHolder.swift` | Modify | Add `setPlaybackRate(_:)` method |\\n| `Features/Browser/Views/BrowserScreen.swift` | Modify | Add `.onChange(of: settings.playbackSpeed)` |\\n| `Features/Browser/Services/ScriptInjector.swift` | Modify | Remove `!= 1.0` conditional guard (~line 650) |\\n\\nNo new files needed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Edge Cases & Risks\\n\\n- **Shorts async video loading:** The video element may not exist when JS fires. The `if(v)` null check handles this; the on-load injection in `ScriptInjector` catches it when the element appears.\\n- **Multiple video elements:** `querySelector('video')` returns the first match. If a page has multiple `<video>` tags, only the first gets the rate. Use `querySelectorAll` + loop if this becomes an issue, but YouTube pages typically have one primary video element.\\n- **Rapid speed changes (dragging the pill):** `evaluateJavaScript` calls are fire-and-forget and lightweight — rapid calls won't cause issues. SwiftUI's `.onChange` coalesces naturally.\\n- **Speed value of 1.0:** With the guard removed, we always inject — this is correct because it resets any previously injected non-1x rate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## What Does NOT Need to Change\\n\\n- **`SpeedPill.swift`** — Already writes to `settings.playbackSpeed`; no changes needed\\n- **`SpeedSelectorSheet.swift`** — Same; already writes to `settings.playbackSpeed`\\n- **`SettingsStore.swift`** — Already persists `playbackSpeed` to `UserDefaults` via `didSet`\\n- **`BrowserActionDrawerView.swift`** — Just hosts the pill; no changes needed\\n- **No new DI/dependency wiring** — all connections already exist", "status": "completed", "node_id": "plan", "node_name": "Plan", "node_type": "claude_agent", "duration_s": 25.5, "started_at": "2026-03-16T22:03:21.300172+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-16T22:03:46.761201+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "dev_result=https://github.com/bakicelebi/talavo/pull/891", "status": "completed", "node_id": "develop", "node_name": "Develop", "node_type": "claude_agent", "duration_s": 94.4, "started_at": "2026-03-16T22:03:46.815818+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-16T22:05:21.226784+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "review_result=**APPROVED**\\n\\nThe #878 changes are clean, correct, and well-scoped:\\n\\n1. **Live-update mechanism**: `SpeedPill` binds to `$settings.playbackSpeed` (a `@Published` property on `SettingsStore`). `BrowserScreen.onChange(of: settings.playbackSpeed)` immediately calls `setPlaybackRate()` on both `VideoWebViewHolder` and `ShortsWebViewHolder` — this ensures the video playback rate updates in real time when the user adjusts the speed pill.\\n\\n2. **Persistence across videos**: `SettingsStore.playbackSpeed` is backed by `UserDefaults`, so the speed persists across app sessions. `ScriptInjector` now always injects the playback speed (removed the `!= 1.0` guard), ensuring the persisted speed is applied to every new video — including returning to 1x explicitly.\\n\\n3. **Memory safety**: `setPlaybackRate` uses optional chaining on `webView?` (which is already `weak` in both holders). No retain cycle concerns.\\n\\n4. **Haptic feedback** on SpeedPill tap/drag is a nice UX touch and follows UIKit patterns correctly.\\n\\n5. **No force unwraps**, proper access control, code is minimal and focused on the issue requirements., review_verdict=APPROVED", "status": "completed", "node_id": "review", "node_name": "Review", "node_type": "claude_agent", "duration_s": 89.7, "started_at": "2026-03-16T22:05:21.282002+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-16T22:06:50.944079+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "review_verdict=APPROVED → done", "status": "completed", "node_id": "review_gate", "node_name": "Review Gate", "node_type": "condition", "duration_s": 0.0, "started_at": "2026-03-16T22:06:50.996875+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-03-16T22:06:51.044839+00:00"}, {"error": null, "output": "Issue #878 implemented and reviewed. PR ready to merge.", "status": "completed", "node_id": "done", "node_name": "Ready to Merge", "node_ty